= i
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A finite extension $L/K$ of non-Archimedean <local fields> is an <unramified extension> when its <ramification index> is one and its <residue-field degree> equals $[L:K]$. Equivalently, its maximal ideal is generated by a <uniformizer> of $K$ and its residue-field extension is separable.

A finite extension of <number fields> is an <everywhere unramified extension of number fields> when every nonzero prime ideal of $K$ is unramified in $L$. Under the convention that includes infinite places, one also requires every real embedding of $K$ to remain real.

= ii
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <Hilbert class field> $\widetilde K$ is the maximal everywhere unramified <abelian extension> of the <number field> $K$, with complete splitting at real places if infinite places are included. <Global class field theory> gives the canonical <Artin reciprocity map>
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Cl}(K)\cong\operatorname{Gal}(\widetilde K/K),}
$$
so $[\widetilde K:K]$ is the <class number> of $K$.

= iii
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Put $K=\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-6})$. Its <fundamental discriminant> is $-24$. The <Minkowski bound for ideal classes> is
$$
\frac{2}{\pi}\sqrt{24}<4,
$$
so every <ideal class> contains an integral ideal of norm $1$, $2$ or $3$. The primes above $2$ and $3$ are ramified, their classes have order at most two, and
$$
(\sqrt{-6})=\mathfrak p_2\mathfrak p_3
$$
shows that they represent the same class. This class is nontrivial because the <norm form> $a^2+6b^2$ does not represent $2$. Hence
$$
\operatorname{Cl}(K)\cong C_2.
$$

Now let $H=K(\sqrt2)=\mathbb Q(\sqrt2,\sqrt{-3})$. The <discriminant of a biquadratic field> is the product of the discriminants of its three quadratic subfields, so
$$
d_H=8\cdot(-3)\cdot(-24)=576=d_K^2.
$$
The <relative discriminant> $\mathfrak D_{H/K}$ therefore has norm one, proving that $H/K$ is unramified at every finite prime. Since $[H:K]=2=|\operatorname{Cl}(K)|$, the <Hilbert class field of Q of square root minus six> is
$$
\boxed{\widetilde K=\mathbb Q(\sqrt2,\sqrt{-3}).}
$$

